DEAD CODE
---------
Sections that remain dead even if all commented-out endpoints are reactivated:

1. Core SQL schemas
   Location:  schemas.py (33 lines)
   Reason:    Defined with Table() objects but never imported anywhere.
              ORM models in orm_models.py are the actual source of truth.

2. Alembic migration
   Location:  alembic/versions/9c0bc8230f3c_init_schema.py (59 lines)
   Reason:    Exists but db.py uses Base.metadata.create_all() for
              auto-migration; Alembic is never initialized.

3. Frontend abstraction
   Location:  frontend.py (26 lines)
   Reason:    FrontendInterface and AngularMock defined but get_frontend()
              is never called from main.py.

4. get_frontend() function
   Location:  main.py:18-19
   Reason:    Defined and never invoked; dead helper function.

5. get_sqlite_file() function
   Location:  config.py:16-19
   Reason:    Defined but never called; get_database_url() constructs the
              path differently.

6. KanbanData.get_columns_by_board()
   Location:  models.py:55-56
   Reason:    Service has its own implementation at service.py:52-57;
              this method is never called.


DUPLICATE IMPLEMENTATIONS
-------------------------
1. Three schema definition styles (schemas.py, orm_models.py, Alembic migration)
2. Repeated load/save pattern in JsonStorage (16 methods)
3. Task mapping logic duplicated across storage methods
4. KanbanData.get_columns_by_board() vs service implementation


DESIGN ISSUES
-------------
1. Thin Service Layer
   Current:   KanbanService wraps StorageInterface 1:1
   Problem:   No business logic, just pass-through + null checks
   Impact:    Extra indirection with no value; could call storage directly

2. Excessive Interface
   Current:   StorageInterface declares 18 abstract methods
   Problem:   Forces both implementations to duplicate identical signatures
   Impact:    Adding new entity requires editing 3 files; too granular

3. Domain/DTO Coupling
   Current:   Board, Column, Task dataclasses used everywhere
   Problem:   Domain models leak into API responses and tests
   Impact:    Changing domain model breaks API contract; tight coupling

4. Multiple Config Paths
   Current:   KANBAN_STORAGE_BACKEND, KANBAN_DATABASE_URL, KANBAN_DATA_DIR
   Problem:   Overlapping configuration for storage selection
   Impact:    Confusion about precedence; maintenance burden


SIGNS OF MULTIPLE DEVELOPERS
----------------------------
The codebase shows signs of at least 5 different contributors with divergent
philosophies:

| Persona           | Contributions                              | Evidence                                    |
|-------------------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| "The Architect"   | Created abstractions                       | StorageInterface, KanbanService,            |
|                   |                                            | FrontendInterface - heavy pattern emphasis  |
| "The Pragmatist"  | Commented out endpoints                    | Left ~50 lines dead with notes              |
|                   |                                            | ("probably shouldn't use this")             |
| "The ORM Migrator"| Created 3 schema styles                   | Started Core, switched ORM, added Alembic   |
|                   |                                            | but still using auto-migrate                |
| "The Frontend Dev"| Built scaffolding                          | frontend.py with AngularMock               |
|                   |                                            | planned but abandoned without removal        |
| "The Ops Contributor"| Wrote isolated tooling                   | cli.py uses argparse + direct instantiation|
|                   |                                            | separate from FastAPI app patterns          |

Interpretation: This codebase reflects either a team with very different
coding philosophies, or a single developer who went through distinct phases
without cleaning up previous approaches. The architectural purity of early
abstractions was gradually abandoned in favor of pragmatic shortcuts, leaving
behind a patchwork of intentions.


